intro={
'ch01-recurrence.md':'''A triangular array can be built one row at a time: put 1 at each end, then combine two neighbours above each interior position. The parameter $m$ controls the two weights. When those weights become negative, ordinary one-step positivity induction stops working. The question is whether a predictable sign pattern nevertheless survives for all rows, rather than just the rows a computer can list. Here “positive even row” means an even row index $n>0$, not an extra assumption about its entries.\n\nThe polynomial $R_{m,n}$ simply packages a row as its coefficients. Evaluating it at 1 adds the entries; evaluating it at $-1$ alternates their signs. Its roots encode different information, so positive coefficients alone do not settle the growth of the largest root.\n\n''',
'ch02-signs.md':'''The proof works two rows at a time. A positive even row feeds a negative odd interior, and two negative factors then feed a positive next even interior. The delicate positions sit next to the boundary: one predecessor there is the fixed value 1. Lemma 2 handles exactly that obstacle instead of pretending the same sign argument works everywhere. Reflection means the left-hand calculation also handles the right-hand edge.\n\n''',
'ch03-identities.md':'''Summing the recurrence is easier than computing each entry. A value in the old row contributes to two positions in the new one, and its two weights add to the same number regardless of its position. That turns a triangular computation into a one-variable product. For even rows, mirror positions have opposite parity, so their alternating contributions cancel in pairs.\n\nThe root annulus asks a different question: where can a polynomial with positive coefficients vanish? Consecutive coefficient ratios give two radii. This is a finite-row bound, with radii that can change with $n$; it is not a uniform annulus for every rescaled root in every row.\n\n''',
'ch04-alternating-sums.md':'''An even row cancels at $y=-1$ by reflection. Odd rows have a middle position, so the same pairing argument leaves something over and does not prove nonvanishing. The manuscript uses stronger arithmetic obstructions: an odd integer cannot be zero, and an integer congruent to 1 modulo 3 cannot be zero. A generating function is a way to keep the whole family of rows in a single formal series; extracting its coefficients recovers the original recurrence.\n\n''',
'ch05-roots.md':'''A root far from zero feels the leading coefficients most strongly. Reflection identifies those coefficients with the first columns of the triangle. Usually the second column supplies the largest exponential scale and the rescaled root equation becomes linear. At $m=-3$, the third column takes over: the correct limiting equation is quadratic. This change is why substituting $m=-3$ into a guessed universal growth law gives the wrong answer.\n\nThe proof below uses Rouché’s theorem, a complex-analysis tool: if a perturbation is smaller than a comparison polynomial on a closed contour, the two polynomials have the same number of zeros inside it. The argument must both produce roots near the predicted locations and exclude roots farther away; one direction alone would not establish the largest modulus.\n\n''',
'ch06-coverage.md':'''Read the following table as the manuscript’s coverage of the cited source claims. “Proved” here means a written argument in this manuscript. In this Mathesis reading, all eight registered results have WRITTEN PROOF status and none has an exact frozen Lean citation. The public comparison candidates at the current [book pin](trureturing:commit) establish narrower integer statements, as detailed after the table. The imported Mathesis nodes remain in their recorded preparing state.\n\n'''}
for name,para in intro.items():
 p=b/'en'/name;head,rest=p.read_text().split('\n',1);p.write_text(head+'\n\n'+para+rest.lstrip())
